    <p>Consider usable floor space, walking routes, chair access and the plants near the door. Aesthetic and practical reasons can both be valid; there is no single required rearrangement.</p>
    <h2>Activity 2 — New Bed and Desk</h2>
    <p>Use Tyler’s L-shaped room above. His bed is <strong>3 ft by 6 ft</strong> and his desk is <strong>3 ft by 2 ft</strong>. On grid paper, choose and label a scale, draw both footprints at that scale, and try arrangements. You may cut out the footprints or draw your own diagram; offer pre-cut first-party pieces if needed.</p>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 115 165" width="115" height="165" role="img" aria-label="Independent bed footprint,3 feet wide and6 feet long">
            <title>Bed footprint</title><rect x="30" y="25" width="60" height="120" fill="#b9d5e8" stroke="#183d52" /><text x="60" y="18" text-anchor="middle">3 ft</text><text x="3" y="90">6 ft</text><text x="60" y="85" text-anchor="middle">bed</text>
        </svg>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 115 85" width="115" height="85" role="img" aria-label="Independent desk footprint,3 feet wide and2 feet deep">
            <title>Desk footprint</title><rect x="30" y="25" width="60" height="40" fill="#e7d9b9" stroke="#183d52" /><text x="60" y="18" text-anchor="middle">3 ft</text><text x="3" y="50">2 ft</text><text x="60" y="50" text-anchor="middle">desk</text>
        </svg>
    </div>
    <p>Work in a group of two or four. Solve both original questions: how should Tyler arrange the bed and desk, and what room area is not covered by furniture? State which footprints you subtract, and explain how you treat the undimensioned closet. Make a labelled poster showing calculations and reasons. The source explicitly allows your own diagram on grid paper as an alternative to its cut-out handout.</p>
    <p>Gallery walk: half the groups stand with posters and explain while the others visit and ask questions. Use a five-minute round, move to another poster after about two minutes, then swap roles and repeat. Your teacher checks the actual designs and discussions.</p>
